#?冽銝??隞?
uploads/
# B4.3: was a bare `media/`, which git matches at ANY depth — it was silently
# swallowing public/media/ (B4.2 had to `git add -f` the demo asset). That is a
# DEPLOY hazard, not tidiness: .dockerignore has no media rule, so a deploy from
# a developer machine bundles the untracked files and looks fine, while a deploy
# from a fresh clone or CI ships broken media. Root-anchored so it still covers
# an upload dir at the repo root and nothing else. public/media was the only
# media/ directory in the repo when this was narrowed.
/media/

